<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  TruePad Android — the whole attack surface this app offers the rest of the
  device. Read it as a list of what is deliberately ABSENT.

  ONE capability-granting permission, and only one: CAMERA. It exists solely to
  scan a receive-code QR (Sealed Pad Transfer) — a QR the other person shows,
  read in-process and discarded. NOT INTERNET, not storage, not network state.
  The app still has no backend; every file it touches is either app-private or
  one the operator picked in the system file picker, which grants access per-URI
  without a permission. Any OTHER permission added later is a visible diff and a
  failing test (ManifestHardeningTest / verifyReleaseManifest), which allow only
  CAMERA and the signature-level self-permission — never INTERNET.

  NO exported component except the launcher activity, which must be exported to
  appear in the launcher at all. No service, no receiver, and no provider OF
  TRUEPAD'S OWN.

  COMPONENTS THAT ARRIVE BY MERGE — the whole list, read back off the built
  release APK rather than recalled, because a partial list reads as a complete
  one:

    activity  dev.systemslibrarian.truepad.app.MainActivity      (ours; the launcher)
    activity  com.google.android.gms.common.api.GoogleApiActivity (Play Services)
    service   androidx.camera.core.impl.MetadataHolderService     (CameraX)
    service   com.google.mlkit.common.internal.MlKitComponentDiscoveryService
    provider  androidx.startup.InitializationProvider
    provider  com.google.mlkit.common.internal.MlKitInitProvider

  MlKitInitProvider is not removable: deleting it was tried, and ML Kit then
  throws "MlKitContext has not been initialized", so it is load-bearing rather
  than incidental. Everything above except our own launcher is
  android:exported="false", and ManifestHardeningTest pins the exported flag on
  every one of them plus the providers' package-scoped authorities by name.

  Three MORE components were merged in and have been DELETED — see the
  datatransport block below. They were a telemetry uploader. The property that matters is unchanged and is the reason the
  app has no FileProvider: there is no authority that can resolve a URI into the
  pad store. An earlier version of this comment said "NO PROVIDER" flatly, which
  stopped being true the moment the scanner dependency changed.

  NO FileProvider. Export writes through the Storage Access Framework to a
  location the operator chose, and sharing an encrypted message uses ACTION_SEND
  with plain text. Neither needs a content provider, so the app does not have
  one — there is no paths XML to get subtly wrong and no way to hand another app
  a URI into the pad store.

  NO intent-filter that accepts content. The app never advertises itself as a
  handler for a file type or a share target. A hostile app therefore cannot push
  anything into it; the operator pulls, through the picker they opened.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      CAMERA — the ONLY capability-granting permission. It is used exclusively to
      scan a receive-code QR: the camera frames are analysed in-process by ML Kit
      and discarded, they never leave the device (there is no INTERNET to carry
      them), and the camera is bound to the scan screen's own lifecycle so it is
      released the instant that screen leaves. The feature is NOT required, so a
      device with no camera still installs and uses the paste path.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.any" android:required="false" />

    <!--
      REMOVED, NOT MERELY UNDECLARED.

      The barcode scanner is ML Kit's BUNDLED model, chosen because ZXing could
      not resolve a 139-module code off another phone's screen on real hardware.
      That library arrives with the Google Play Services client stack
      (play-services-basement/-base/-tasks) and Google's datatransport stack
      attached. The permissions come from datatransport, not from Play Services
      proper: transport-backend-cct declares INTERNET and ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE,
      transport-runtime declares ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE. (play-services-basement
      declares none; an earlier version of this comment blamed it.) Manifest
      merging is a union: a library permission lands in the shipping APK whether
      or not this file mentions it, and the release APK was in fact built asking
      for both before these two lines existed.

      Saying "TruePad has no network" in a comment does not make it so once a
      dependency says otherwise. These entries delete both permissions from the
      merged result, so the guarantee is enforced by the OS — the process cannot
      open a socket even if some transitive code path tries — rather than
      asserted by documentation. The scanner keeps working because the model is
      an asset inside the APK (assets/mlkit_barcode_models/*.tflite): nothing is
      fetched, so nothing needs a network to fetch it. That is verified on a
      handset, not assumed, and verifyReleaseManifest fails the build if either
      permission ever reappears.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" tools:node="remove" />

    <application
        android:name=".TruePadApp"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.TruePad"
        android:supportsRtl="true"

        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:hasFragileUserData="false"

        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"

        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        tools:targetApi="33">

        <!--
          THE ONLY EXPORTED COMPONENT, and only because a launcher entry
          requires it. It takes no data: the intent filter is MAIN/LAUNCHER and
          nothing else, so there is no path by which another application can
          hand this activity a URI, an extra, or an action of its own choosing.

          configChanges is deliberately NOT set. Letting Android recreate the
          activity on rotation is the honest default and is what the lifecycle
          tests exercise — authoritative state is reloaded from the engine on
          every resume, never restored from a saved UI bundle.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.TruePad"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          REMOVED, not inherited. androidx.profileinstaller (pulled in
          transitively by Compose) merges in an EXPORTED broadcast receiver so
          that shell tooling can trigger baseline-profile installation. It is
          permission-protected, and it is still an exported component this app
          neither needs nor uses — TruePad ships no baseline profile and has no
          benchmark harness. An exported component that exists for a feature you
          do not have is surface for nothing, so it is removed here and
          ManifestHardeningTest fails if it ever comes back.

          The provider that installs the profile at startup is left in place: it
          is not exported, and removing it would disable the ahead-of-time
          profile the app does benefit from.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="androidx.profileinstaller.ProfileInstallReceiver"
            tools:node="remove"
            tools:ignore="MissingClass" />
    
        <!--
          ML KIT'S TELEMETRY UPLOADER, DELETED.

          Inspecting the built release APK turned up
          MLKitLoggingOptions{libraryName=common, enableFirelog=true}, a
          CctBackendFactory (Google's Clearcut transport), a JobScheduler service
          and an AlarmManager receiver. That is a scheduled uploader, switched on
          by default, merged into an app whose About screen tells the operator
          there is no analytics and no crash reporting. Nobody chose it; it
          arrived with the barcode decoder.

          Removing INTERNET already means nothing can reach the network. These
          three entries remove the uploader's ENTRY POINTS as well, so no job is
          ever scheduled and no broadcast ever wakes it. The classes remain in the
          APK — ML Kit references them directly and dropping the artifact fails R8
          with eight missing-class errors — but nothing can start them.

          ScannerOfflineTest proves on a handset that decoding still works with
          these gone, which is the only reason they are safe to remove.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="com.google.android.datatransport.runtime.backends.TransportBackendDiscovery"
            tools:node="remove" />
        <service
            android:name="com.google.android.datatransport.runtime.scheduling.jobscheduling.JobInfoSchedulerService"
            tools:node="remove" />
        <receiver
            android:name="com.google.android.datatransport.runtime.scheduling.jobscheduling.AlarmManagerSchedulerBroadcastReceiver"
            tools:node="remove" />
</application>
</manifest>
